# Defect 216 (panel 188, stage D, 2026-10-03): a control character in a group
# head's string, past the NUL and the line ends that nothing carries. Clang,
# the linker and `pkg-config` carry each one (the sitting's sweeps: of every
# byte 0x01 to 0x7F only LF, CR and `>` fail an `#include <...>`), so the
# refusal is a thesis rule; no message can show one, so the message names it
# by its code point. The panel's survey found none in 103,736 header names
# and 2,600 library and `.pc` names; a name holding one is what would make
# this rule wrong. Only `\t` is written here, the one control character an
# escape spells; the raw bytes are the sitting's runner cases. In a package's
# string a tab is where `pkg-config` splits its argument, which nothing
# carries, so it is told as that.
#
# 2026-10-08, defect 445: no NTFS name holds a byte from U+0001 to U+001F
# (Win32 error 123 on the Windows box), so the tab in a header and a library
# is `unwritable_name`, told before the thesis rule, and `check --permissive`
# refuses it; U+200B and U+2028 stand in a file's name there and stay.

extern "a\tb.h"    #~ unwritable_name
    function h1() -> i32

extern "stdio.h" link "m\tx"    #~ unwritable_name
    function h2() -> i32

extern "stdio.h" package "zlib\tzlib"    #~ unwritable_name
    function h3() -> i32

# Stage E: the next line holds U+200B, a zero width space, between `std` and
# `io.h`, Unicode's Default_Ignorable_Code_Point; with no such header the
# message would have read as if `stdio.h` were missing. The line after holds
# U+2028, a line separator, which clang's message prints as `<U+2028>`.
extern "std​io.h"    #~ unshowable_name
    function h4() -> i32

extern "std io.h"    #~ unshowable_name
    function h5() -> i32

function main()
    print(h1() + h2() + h3() + h4() + h5())
